                              Brute Force
 class Solution {
    public ListNode oddEvenList(ListNode head) {
        if (head == null) return null;

        ListNode oddDummy = new ListNode(0);
        ListNode evenDummy = new ListNode(0);

        ListNode oddTail = oddDummy;
        ListNode evenTail = evenDummy;

        ListNode curr = head;
        int pos = 1;

        while (curr != null) {
            if (pos % 2 == 1) {
                oddTail.next = new ListNode(curr.val);
                oddTail = oddTail.next;
            } else {
                evenTail.next = new ListNode(curr.val);
                evenTail = evenTail.next;
            }

            curr = curr.next;
            pos++;
        }

        oddTail.next = evenDummy.next;
        return oddDummy.next;
    }
}

                            Intuition
A simple approach is to create two separate linked lists:
One list stores all nodes at odd positions.
The other list stores all nodes at even positions.
Traverse the original linked list while keeping track of the current position.
Append each node's value to the appropriate list.
After the traversal is complete, connect the odd list to the beginning of the even list.
This approach is easy to understand but creates new nodes, requiring additional memory.

                          Time Complexity
Time Complexity: O(n)
